  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66297,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 470,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "<p>Affected individuals are predominantly children and young adults. Approximately 5% of carriers develop their first neoplasm by the time they are 10&nbsp;years old (females: 4%; males: 7%). The average incidence of neoplasms by the age of 50&nbsp;years is 19%, with substantially higher risk in females (females: 27%; males: 10%). Gynaecological neoplasms predominate in older patients {30715996}. Key clinical phenotypes associated with germline <em>DICER1</em> pathogenic variants are presented in <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/attachment/34/470/15208\" target=\"_blank\">#15208</a>Table&nbsp;<a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/attachment/34/470/15208\" target=\"_blank\">#15208</a>.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-10T14:10:31"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66298,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 470,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The incidence is unknown.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:43:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66299,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 470,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>DICER1</em> syndrome is caused by heritable pathogenic mutations in <em>DICER1</em> {19556464}, which may also arise de&nbsp;novo or cause somatic mosaicism {26475046}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:43:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66300,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 470,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>DICER1 is an RNase&nbsp;III endoribonuclease and is essential for processing microRNAs in the RNA interference pathway. Altered function of DICER1 disrupts processing of the hairpin-shaped precursor microRNAs, resulting in an excess of 3p microRNAs and a deficiency of 5p microRNAs, leading to aberrant posttranscriptional regulation of multiple cancer gene networks {25176334}. Somatic mutations occur in non-epithelial ovarian neoplasms, especially Sertoli&ndash;Leydig cell tumour {22187960}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:43:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66301,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 470,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The macroscopic appearance is nonspecific.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:43:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66302,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 470,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Sertoli&ndash;Leydig cell tumours in patients with <em>DICER1</em> germline mutations are often moderately to poorly differentiated {28654427; 22187960}. Difficult-to-classify or mixed sex cord&ndash;stromal tumours may be a clue to <em>DICER1</em> syndrome. Other rare tumours include gynandroblastoma and cervical embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma. These tumours are not histologically distinct from their non-syndromic counterparts.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:43:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66303,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 470,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:43:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66304,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 470,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Pathogenic mutation of <em>DICER1</em> must be confirmed.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:43:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
